        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Bristol',
            subtitle: 'Card game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Bristol is a patience (solitaire) card game played with a single standard 52-card deck. It is a fan-type layout in the style of La Belle Lucie, with building regardless of suit on both the foundations and the tableau, and is considered relatively easy to win.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Country/Region of Origin: United States\nTime Period: Around 1950\nHistorical Development: Invented by the card game authors Morehead and Mott-Smith as a fan-type patience closely related to La Belle Lucie.\nCultural Significance: Known as an easy-to-win solitaire, included in classic card game collections.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Minimum Players: 2\nMaximum Players: 6\nOptimal Players: 4\nPartnerships: Optional',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Type: Standard 52-card deck\nNumber of Decks: 1\nJokers: Not included',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Standard 52-card deck\nScore sheet (optional)\nTable space',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Cards per Player: Varies by player count\nDealing Pattern: Clockwise\n--',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Belvedere: Belvedere is a closely related patience in which an ace is removed from the deck before the deal and immediately set up as a foundation, but play otherwise follows the normal rules of Bristol.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'House rules and table variants: Players sometimes adjust small details such as redeal limits or whether gaps in the tableau may be filled, but core Bristol structure and building rules stay the same.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
